<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johan Gustaf Renat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1682">1682</a>–<a href="/years/1744">1744</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johan Gustaf Renat was a Swedish artillery officer captured by the Russians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> in 1709 and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. Captured again by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> in 1716, he spent nearly twenty years among them, teaching them to cast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> and to manufacture other goods. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1734 with maps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> that were valuable to European geographers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-gustaf-renat.jpg" alt="Johan Gustaf Renat" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johan Gustaf Renat · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Renat_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
